./LOGBOOK.md:24:- ROOT CAUSE/FIX: `measureRealHEVLifecycle` retained every sample in an initially empty Swift `Array` after taking its baseline. The observer now reserves the exact bounded cycle count before the baseline snapshot. A production-call-site regression asserts capacity for 1000 samples, and the diagnostic entry accepts only 100/500/1000 cycles; malformed or wider values fail closed.
./LOGBOOK.md:26:- RULE: A fresh production-entry repeat proved 100 cycles insufficient: it stopped fail-closed on one `+65,536 B` increase and no release. The simplest repeatable gate therefore uses the next predeclared row, 500 cycles, after fixed 10-sample warmup. It permits a full plateau or a measured release only while maximum drawup stays within 256 KiB (1% of the upper 25 MiB target); late unreleased rise and a released-but-over-ceiling linear sawtooth fail closed. The earlier 2000-cycle/trailing-1000 rule measured observer allocation and is retired rather than widened further.
./LOGBOOK.md:59:- RUNTIME GATE: Rework 04 keeps complete classification of exact `App/`, `Sources/`, and `relay/` roots and uses language-specific lexical tokens. C-family and Go block comments terminate at the first `*/`; Swift preserves nested comments, escaped identifiers, and raw-string interpolation. C alternative `%:%:` token paste, Swift inferred `.init(contentsOf:)`, and all prior direct/reference/alias/reflection/import forms reject while explicit local `URL(fileURLWithPath:)` reads and documentation-only comments/raw strings remain allowed. Unknown/new kinds, symlinks, and non-regular entries still fail closed.
./LOGBOOK.md:114:- MAINTENANCE: BSD-3-Clause and Apache-2.0 notices remain mandatory; archive, patch, header, artifact, extension-safety, and negative source gates fail closed. `TASK-260728-3cveay` owns future selected-engine fork/rebase work. Any mandatory red, security advisory, patch-bound breach, platform/server-policy change, or red M3 physical result reopens ADR-014 rather than weakening a gate.
./LOGBOOK.md:256:- VALIDATION: XcodeGen regeneration is byte-stable. Plist and shell checks, strict Swift format, four Swift Testing cases, the complete unsigned host/provider graph, and all 378 existing SwiftPM tests pass. The signed archive contains the exact team, host/provider identifiers, approved profile UUIDs, unsuffixed `packet-tunnel-provider` entitlements, Apple Development signatures, arm64 binaries, and exactly one provider. Five negative mutations prove identifier, capability, profile, nesting, and signature drift fail closed.
./LOGBOOK.md:451:- DECISION: coverage runs **in both directions** — an entitlement key named literally in any assertion must be registered, and a registered scope whose assertion stopped naming the key fails. That is what makes the class fail closed rather than this one pair. All 10 scope-bearing entitlement assertions and all 3 profile assertions registered, not just the two reported.
./LOGBOOK.md:453:- REGRESSION (in the new gate, caught by its own negative mutations): R35's mention scan indexed `assertion_text[aid]` and raised `KeyError` when a scope pointed at a deleted assertion — crashing the run and **silently suppressing four pre-existing `[A-set]` gates** that expected R27. A gate must fail closed with a message, never by raising. Fixed to `.get`.
./LOGBOOK.md:470:- FINDING (**a gate never observed failing is an assumption**): r6 introduced the board gate and only ever ran it in the passing direction, so nothing proved it would fail closed. 13 board negative gates now drive it from a *simulated* record — no live board record is mutated to test a gate — and each failure must be attributed to the **drifted consumer's own rule**, or a mutation to one consumer gets credited by a failure in the other.
./LOGBOOK.md:560:- VERIFICATION: 24 rules / 537 checks over the JSON, exit 0, plus **23 negative gates** that each re-introduce a defect and must fail closed naming their rule (6 for the NE-channel defect, 4 App Group, 3 allowlist, 3 sandbox attribution, 7 re-checking preserved r1 decisions). The r1 harness mutated the JSON with regex substitution, which can silently no-op; r2 mutates the parsed object, so a mutation that fails to apply is itself reported as a gate failure.
./LOGBOOK.md:573:- VERIFICATION: `validate_matrix.py` runs 18 rules / 247 checks over the JSON contract, exit 0; nine deliberate mutations (broken containment, legacy-prefixed identifier, wrong NE value, granted pending-keychain row, unprefixed macOS group, collapsed probe group, C1-authorized distribution profile, iOS marked provisioned, sysex-install on the provider) each fail closed with the expected rule.
./LOGBOOK.md:640:- FINDING: The regression guard proves only printable ASCII plus integer-valued numbers. A schema-valid expansion setting `device.installedMemoryBytes=9007199254740993` passes that guard, but jq 1.8.1 preserves the unsafe integer while RFC 8785/ECMAScript serialization uses the binary64 value `9007199254740992`; hashes diverge (`3de3ad9f…` versus `30a40240…`). The guard therefore does not fail closed when the fixture numeric domain expands.
./LOGBOOK.md:802:- PROVENANCE REWORK: Release entry points now require offline-provisioned official Go and Syft archives retained beside their installed tools. They recheck the accepted host-specific archive hash, canonical path-free receipt, installed archive members, exact Go 1.26.5 host identity with `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, and Syft 1.48.0 commit `3e2bc6ed095f7ec1a415fb38cfe1c319e95dfed6`; missing, older/newer, wrong-platform, wrong-commit, wrong-checksum, auto-toolchain, and version-only inputs fail closed.
