    15	
    16	## 1. Scope and non-claims
    17	
    18	This contract governs credential-free pull-request validation for the
    19	macOS-first prototype and the protected macOS/relay release path. It defines
    20	workflow boundaries and blocking check names; it does not implement GitHub
    21	Actions.
    22	
    23	- Gate A0 is not an input to prototype CI. It re-arms before iOS submission or
    24	  any public distribution claim that depends on App Review acceptance.
    25	- No Linux CI runner is required for the working-client path. The relay's four
    26	  declared Darwin/Linux, amd64/arm64 outputs may be cross-built on the pinned
    27	  macOS toolchain. Native/equivalent release evidence remains owned by the relay
    28	  release contract and cannot be inferred from cross-compilation.
    29	- iOS targets, TestFlight, App Store Connect, and App Review workflows are
    30	  deferred with iOS. Their future checks are named for traceability but are not
    31	  required checks on the macOS-only path and must never be reported as passed.
    32	- Credential-free compile and static inspection are not signing, notarization,
    33	  installation, Network Extension activation, VPN lifecycle, or publication
    34	  evidence.
    35	- The build host must not install or activate a system extension or VPN app,
    36	  persist VPN preferences, call `startVPNTunnel`, or mutate routes or DNS.
    37	
    38	## 2. Trust invariants
    39	
    40	1. Untrusted source is any fork pull request, pull-request head, arbitrary
    41	   branch, mutable tag, downloaded artifact without a verified digest, or
    42	   rerun whose candidate identity differs from its original attempt.
    43	2. Repository source becomes release input only as an exact commit reachable
    44	   from the protected default branch and bound into an immutable candidate
    45	   manifest. A tag is a label, never identity authority.
    46	3. Workflow code that accepts a release candidate or can reach production
    47	   credentials is loaded only from the protected default branch.
    48	   `release-prepare.yml` is triggered only when the dedicated release-request
    49	   GitHub App opens a typed issue; GitHub binds the `issues: opened` event to
    50	   the default-branch workflow/ref/SHA. The issue, sender claims, requested
    51	   commit, version, and request ID are untrusted inputs until independently
    52	   resolved. Tag push, `repository_dispatch`, selectable-ref
    53	   `workflow_dispatch`, `workflow_run` from candidate-controlled code, and
    54	   relative reusable-workflow calls are not release entry points.
    55	   `pull_request_target` is prohibited for building, executing, caching, or
    56	   uploading pull-request code.
    57	4. Workflow and job permissions default to `contents: read`; every other
    58	   `GITHUB_TOKEN` permission is `none`. An additional read-only metadata
    59	   permission may be granted without an environment only when its exact API
    60	   call is inventoried. A write permission is granted only to its single call
    61	   site after protected-environment approval. Repository Actions settings are
    62	   a default, not a hard ceiling on same-repository workflow YAML, so no trust
    63	   claim depends on them preventing a workflow from requesting write access.
    64	   No production secret exists at repository or organization scope; protected
    65	   environments restrict deployment refs to the protected default branch.
    66	5. Pull-request jobs have no production environment and receive no certificate,
    67	   private key, certificate password, App Store Connect issuer/key, provisioning
    68	   profile secret, notarization credential, Sparkle private key, feed-origin
    69	   credential, publication token, privileged runner, or route to a private
    70	   production network. GitHub-hosted runners do have public Internet egress;
    71	   that capability is treated as untrusted and never as release authority.
    72	6. Every third-party action is pinned to a reviewed full commit SHA. A mutable
    73	   tag, branch, range, or unreviewed action source fails before execution.
    74	7. Pull-request caches and artifacts are untrusted accelerators. They are never
    75	   release inputs; cache hits are revalidated against source and manifests.
    76	8. Release jobs consume one candidate manifest and exact digests. Reruns may
    77	   reuse that identity but may not resolve a new tag, branch head, dependency,
    78	   cache namespace, or artifact under the old release attempt.
    79	9. Production publication is serialized and feed-last. Cancellation or any
    80	   failed/missing/unknown prerequisite leaves the candidate non-promotable.
    81	10. Absence and read failure are distinct. A missing optional row is `deferred`
    82	    or `not-applicable`; an unreadable or indeterminate required input is
    83	    `unknown` and fails closed.
    84	
    85	## 3. Workflow and job inventory
    86	
    87	All workflow files use explicit top-level and job-level `permissions`, bounded
    88	timeouts, `set -euo pipefail` (or an equivalent fail-fast shell contract),
    89	immutable action pins, and concurrency groups. `persist-credentials: false` is
    90	required on every checkout unless the single publication step documents its
    91	write need. The Network column records both runner capability and authorized
    92	use: a GitHub-hosted runner has public egress even where trusted steps authorize
    93	no post-bootstrap network use.
    94	
