   384	### 6.3 Network and cache rules
   385	
   386	- Standard GitHub-hosted runners have public Internet egress. For untrusted PR
   387	  jobs this is an explicit capability, not an enforceable deny boundary; those
   388	  jobs therefore receive no secret, write token, protected environment,
   389	  privileged runner, private-production route, or promotable output namespace.
   390	- Trusted steps authorize only exact GitHub endpoints, checksum-pinned
   391	  package/tool origins, and advisory databases. Credential-bearing release jobs
   392	  use an enforceable job-specific egress boundary or fail before secret access;
   393	  only the owning job can reach Apple notarization, GitHub publication, relay
   394	  staging, or the Relux update origin.
   395	- Redirects, new hosts, mutable bootstrap responses, proxy injection, TLS
   396	  failure, or inability to prove the protected job's egress boundary fail
   397	  closed. Network inventories are retained in sanitized metadata.
   398	- Fork PRs may restore only read-only caches keyed by trust class, OS image,
   399	  exact tool/dependency/source hashes, and cache schema. They never save to a
   400	  namespace restored by trusted/release jobs.
   401	- Cache contents are never authoritative. Checksums, manifests, generated drift,
   402	  linkage, and artifact inspection run on every hit. Poisoned or unreadable
   403	  caches are discarded and the job rebuilds; inability to rebuild is failure.
