   460	## 9. Approval, disputes, and change control
   461	
   462	The autonomous draft is reviewed by an independent reviewer agent before the
   463	solution-architect handoff. Human security, release, and platform ratification
   464	is intentionally consolidated in `TASK-260717-2d308k`; its pending status does
   465	not block downstream implementation of this accepted draft, but publication
   466	cannot pass `release/review-gate` without its recorded verdict.
   467	
   468	No current trust decision is disputed. The exact retention durations,
   469	environment names, endpoint allowlists, and whether the separately protected
   470	trusted-branch SARIF publisher is enabled are policy-fill decisions because the
   471	source specs require those controls but do not fix their exact values. They were
   472	selected to produce a testable least-privilege contract after checking the
   473	task's explicit non-scope: they do not reinterpret A0, require Linux CI for the prototype,
   474	activate iOS, issue credentials, implement workflows, or make legal/App Review
   475	decisions. Human ratification may narrow them; any broadening requires a written
   476	threat-model delta, accountable security/release owner, negative fixture, and a
   477	fresh review.
   478	
   479	Any later dispute records: the exact row, current evidence, two or more viable
   480	options, security and release trade-offs, recommendation, accountable owner,
   481	and the decision deadline. Until resolved, the affected required check or
   482	release gate remains failing/unknown; unrelated credential-free work continues.
   483	
   484	Changes to workflow triggers, permissions, action pins, runner classes, cache or
   485	artifact trust, release environments, credential scope, publication order,
   486	retention, provenance schema, or required-check names require review of this
   487	contract and `.spec/threat-model.md` in the same change.
   488	
   489	## 10. External platform basis
   490	
   491	The trusted-entry design follows GitHub's documented execution model: the
   492	`issues` event uses the last default-branch commit/ref and requires its workflow
   493	file on the default branch; creating an issue through the API needs only
   494	fine-grained `Issues: write`; workflow/job YAML can adjust the repository's
   495	default token permissions; GitHub-hosted runners have public Internet access;
   496	and full commit SHAs are the safest reusable-workflow reference. See
   497	[events that trigger workflows](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#issues),
   498	[create issue API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/issues/issues#create-an-issue),
   499	[workflow permission calculation](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#how-permissions-are-calculated-for-a-workflow-job),
   500	[GitHub-hosted runner networking](https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/concepts/runners/private-networking#about-github-hosted-runners-networking),
   501	and [reuse workflows](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/reuse-automations/reuse-workflows).

