    10	## Blocking finding: tag intake is not externally constrained to the declared permissions or network boundary
    11	
    12	The revision fixes the previous self-protecting candidate-acceptance guard by
    13	moving candidate acceptance into a default-branch `workflow_run`. That protected
    14	side is directionally sound. The upstream `v*` tag workflow is still described
    15	as externally guaranteed to be unprivileged, however, while the contract also
    16	acknowledges that GitHub loads arbitrary tagged workflow code:
    17	
    18	- trust invariant 4 (contract lines 53–60) says repository Actions settings
    19	  enforce a read-only default so tagged workflow code cannot self-elevate;
    20	- §3.3 lines 176–194 says arbitrary tagged code has only `contents: read`, no
    21	  cache/artifact upload, no publication permission, and `Network: None`;
    22	- the PlantUML edge repeats “arbitrary tagged workflow code / no secrets,
    23	  cache, artifacts, or authority.”
    24	
    25	Those are not enforceable properties of a standard same-repository tag-push
    26	workflow. GitHub's workflow syntax says token permissions start from the
    27	repository/organization default and are then adjusted by the workflow and job
    28	`permissions` keys. GitHub's repository settings documentation is explicit that
    29	a repository writer may add or remove `GITHUB_TOKEN` access in workflow YAML.
    30	The fork-PR write downgrade does not apply to a same-repository tag push. A
    31	tagged workflow can therefore replace `contents: read` with write scopes; the
    32	restricted repository setting is a default, not a hard ceiling. Standard
    33	GitHub-hosted runners also have public Internet access by default, so `Network:
    34	None` cannot be guaranteed by the cited settings. Arbitrary tagged steps can
    35	also attempt cache/artifact or external output even if the trusted downstream
    36	correctly refuses to consume it.
    37	
    38	Official platform evidence:
    39	
    40	- Workflow code is loaded from the event-associated SHA/ref:
    41	  https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/workflows-and-actions/workflows
    42	- Token permissions are calculated from defaults and then adjusted by workflow
    43	  and job YAML:
    44	  https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#how-permissions-are-calculated-for-a-workflow-job
    45	- Repository settings are defaults and writers can modify permissions in
    46	  workflow YAML:
    47	  https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/enabling-features-for-your-repository/managing-github-actions-settings-for-a-repository#setting-the-permissions-of-the-github_token-for-your-repository
    48	- Standard GitHub-hosted runners have public Internet access by default:
    49	  https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/concepts/runners/private-networking#about-github-hosted-runners-networking
    50	
    51	Impact: acceptance criterion 1's network/token inventory and the stated tag
    52	spoofing trust boundary are materially false. A malicious or mistaken tag at a
    53	commit with modified workflow YAML can obtain the GitHub token permissions
    54	allowed to that push workflow and network access before the protected
    55	`workflow_run` boundary. Ignoring its outputs prevents candidate substitution,
    56	but does not make the intake itself read-only, output-free, or network-free.
    57	
    58	Required rework:
    59	
    60	1. Use a trigger whose workflow code is loaded only from a trusted default-branch
    61	   revision (for example a tightly authenticated `repository_dispatch` release
    62	   request), or document a separate externally enforced tag-creation/runner/token
    63	   control that actually caps same-repository tag runs. A repository default
    64	   permission setting alone is insufficient.
    65	2. If tag-push intake remains, inventory its real worst-case capabilities and
    66	   make every cache, artifact, token, network, and runner effect explicitly
    67	   untrusted and non-authoritative. Do not claim those capabilities are absent.
    68	3. Add negative fixtures that mutate tagged YAML to request `write-all`, select
    69	   an unauthorized runner, contact an arbitrary endpoint, and upload/cache
    70	   attacker-controlled bytes. Prove either that an external control rejects the
    71	   run before execution or that every effect remains outside all protected
    72	   release and publication authorities.
    73	4. Update trust invariant 4, §3.3, the threat row, diagram, traceability, and
    74	   LOGBOOK together.
    75	
    76	## Secondary consistency finding
    77	
    78	Trust invariant 4 says every permission other than `contents: read` is `none`
    79	and that a job elevates one named permission only after protected-environment
    80	approval. `release/candidate-identity` (line 218) instead grants
    81	`contents: read` plus `actions: read` with no protected environment. The latter
    82	is a reasonable read-only metadata permission for the GitHub Actions API, but
    83	the invariant must distinguish additional read-only metadata scope from write
    84	elevation so the contract has one testable rule.
