     3	Checked: 2026-08-30
     4	
     5	## Corrected platform boundary
     6	
     7	- `issues: opened` runs the workflow at the last protected-default-branch
     8	  commit/ref and requires the workflow file on the default branch.
     9	- A fine-grained GitHub App installation token needs only `Issues: write` to
    10	  create the request issue.
    11	- The rejected `repository_dispatch` design needed `Contents: write`; that same
    12	  permission also authorizes GitHub Release/asset and Git-ref mutation APIs, so
    13	  a documented endpoint convention could not constrain a stolen token.
    14	- The request issue and sender claims remain untrusted. Trusted workflow code
    15	  binds the captured event payload digest, verifies the expected App sender,
    16	  issue/template/request identity, candidate ancestry, trusted workflow/policy
    17	  digests, fresh check evidence, and an absent intended tag.
    18	
    19	Official sources:
    20	
    21	- https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#issues
    22	- https://docs.github.com/en/rest/issues/issues#create-an-issue
    23	- https://docs.github.com/en/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-dispatch-event
    24	- https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases
    25	- https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/assets
    26	- https://docs.github.com/en/rest/git/refs
    27	
    28	## Result
    29	
    30	The short-lived requester App is scoped to `Issues: write`, not Contents. A
    31	compromise can create/edit/close/reopen/label/spam request issues and comments,
    32	but cannot mutate Contents, Git refs, GitHub Releases/assets, Actions/workflows,
    33	protected environments, or secrets. The release workflow never refetches
    34	mutable issue text as authority. Tag/push, `repository_dispatch`, selectable-ref
    35	dispatch, and candidate-controlled upstream workflow completion are not release
    36	entry points.
