.spec/threat-model.md-226-## 8. Fail-closed and availability semantics
.spec/threat-model.md-227-
.spec/threat-model.md-228-- **Compatible mode** is the only route mode currently admitted by the shared
--
.spec/threat-model.md-230-- **Fail-closed mode** is an accepted platform-scoped design using
.spec/threat-model.md-231-  `includeAllNetworks` where supported, but it is not implemented end to end.
.spec/threat-model.md-232-- Apple may exclude DHCP, captive-portal negotiation, certain cellular or
.spec/threat-model.md-233-  companion-device services, and traffic needed to maintain the VPN. OS behavior
.spec/threat-model.md:234:  can change by release.
.spec/threat-model.md-235-- Mandatory SSH, TCP, safe-DNS, packet-plane, or settings failure must withdraw
.spec/threat-model.md-236-  advertised capability and tear down settings rather than create a physical
.spec/threat-model.md-237-  fallback. The coordinator implements this ownership rule against injected
.spec/threat-model.md:238:  dependencies; real route/DNS evidence is pending.
.spec/threat-model.md-239-- “Kill switch,” “zero leaks,” and “all packets under every condition” are
.spec/threat-model.md-240-  prohibited claims. The approved term is **platform-scoped fail-closed** with
.spec/threat-model.md-241-  the tested OS exceptions named.
