.spec/threat-model.md-243-## 9. Abuse boundaries
.spec/threat-model.md-244-
.spec/threat-model.md:245:- The user supplies the SSH host and credentials. There is no shared exit pool,
.spec/threat-model.md:246:  server marketplace, credential broker, or Relux-operated exit in baseline v1.
.spec/threat-model.md-247-- The product opens outbound `direct-tcpip` channels and relay UDP sockets for
.spec/threat-model.md:248:  client traffic. It does not expose inbound forwarding or unsolicited relays.
.spec/threat-model.md-249-- The internal SOCKS listener is an authenticated implementation boundary, not
.spec/threat-model.md-250-  a supported proxy endpoint for browsers, LAN clients, or third parties.
.spec/threat-model.md-251-- Relay deployment is limited to reviewed exec commands and stdin/stdout. No
.spec/threat-model.md-252-  SFTP, interactive shell, agent forwarding, arbitrary command UI, root daemon,
.spec/threat-model.md-253-  public listener, or firewall automation belongs to the baseline.
.spec/threat-model.md:254:- Association, frame, queue, timer, resolver, channel, memory, and diagnostic
.spec/threat-model.md-255-  limits protect resource availability. They do not certify remote destinations
.spec/threat-model.md-256-  or prevent abuse originating from a compromised client or exit.
