    73	without production credentials. Release workflows use GitHub Environments and
    74	least-privilege secrets; no certificate, private key, issuer credential, or
    75	provisioning secret is committed.
    76	
    77	Required pipelines:
    78	
    79	1. shared-core unit and protocol conformance tests on macOS;
    80	2. macOS host/extension build, entitlement inspection, signing verification,
    81	   notarization, DMG publication, checksums, and stable-name asset;
    82	3. iOS host/extension archive, entitlement/provisioning inspection, and
    83	   TestFlight upload;
    84	4. reproducible relay cross-build matrix, tests, checksums, manifest generation,
    85	   and bundling into Apple targets;
    86	5. dependency license/SBOM and secret scanning;
    87	6. semantic version/tag validation and release notes.
    88	
    89	Production release jobs are serialized. Build artifacts retain exact source
    90	commit, dependency lockfiles, relay manifest, protocol version, bundle versions,
    91	and signing identity metadata.
    92	
    93	## App Review package
    94	
    95	The submission package includes:
    96	
    97	- an accurate VPN data-flow explanation and test SSH host instructions;
    98	- in-app VPN data disclosure and public privacy policy;
    99	- Network Extension entitlement purpose;
   100	- regional licensing notes or excluded storefronts;
   101	- explanation that users provide their own SSH exit host;
   102	- degraded-mode behavior and system-excluded traffic disclosure;
   103	- review account/profile or reproducible setup that does not expose private
   104	  production credentials.
    88	energy, CPU, packet rate, and syscall rate. Later optimizations require a before
    89	and after measurement. A HEV fork is justified only by a demonstrated material
    90	bottleneck and validated regression coverage.
    91	
    92	## Security and release validation
    93	
    94	- host-key first-use/change cases and Keychain access boundaries;
    95	- malformed packet/SOCKS/SSH/relay fuzz targets with bounded allocations;
    96	- log/support-export redaction tests;
    97	- dependency licenses, notices, SBOM, pinned revisions, and relay hash manifest;
    98	- exact entitlements and nested code signatures in archived apps;
    99	- macOS notarization/stapling/Gatekeeper and iOS/TestFlight install;
   100	- privacy disclosure and App Review notes checked against actual behavior.
    40	- Key import validates format and permissions. Key generation uses platform
    41	  cryptography and never exports silently.
    42	- Secrets are not placed in `NETunnelProviderProtocol.providerConfiguration`,
    43	  logs, crash annotations, shell commands, or task-board resources.
    44	- Agent forwarding is disabled.
    45	
    46	## Relay supply chain
    47	
    48	- Relay assets are built from pinned source revisions in CI for the declared
    49	  OS/architecture matrix.
    50	- The application bundle contains a manifest with protocol version, platform,
    51	  file size, and SHA-256 for every asset.
    52	- Upload occurs only after SSH host verification and authentication.
    53	- Remote installation uses private permissions, checksum verification, and
    54	  atomic rename; a mismatch never executes.
    55	- The relay performs a versioned handshake and reports its build identity.
    56	- Third-party notices for HEV, hev-task-system, hev-socks5-core, lwIP, the SSH
    57	  engine, and any relay dependency ship with the product.
    58	
    59	## Traffic and routing safety
    60	
    61	- Ordinary traffic and DNS fail closed on mandatory tunnel failure.
    62	- SSH endpoint routes are narrow and derived from the actual connected endpoint.
    63	- Malformed packet, SOCKS, SSH channel, or relay frames are rejected within
    64	  bounded memory and do not become arbitrary allocations.
    65	- The relay validates address families, lengths, association IDs, and datagram
    66	  limits before opening or using sockets.
    67	- Local internal SOCKS/bridge endpoints are not reachable as a user-configurable
    68	  general proxy.
    69	- QUIC blocking and degraded-mode decisions are explicit capability policies,
    70	  not silent traffic interception.
    71	
    72	## Diagnostics and data collection
    73	
    74	Default logs MAY include timestamps, state transitions, lane IDs, aggregate
    75	counts, error domains/codes, memory measurements, negotiated algorithms, relay
    76	build identity, and redacted IP-family information.
    77	
    78	Default logs MUST NOT include private keys, passphrases, packet payloads, DNS
    79	names, destination hostnames/IPs, full local network addresses, shell command
    80	stdin, or user traffic samples. A user-initiated support export applies a
    81	documented redaction pass and previews included categories.
    82	
    83	The baseline has no analytics or traffic telemetry. Any future telemetry is
    84	opt-in, aggregate-only, documented in the privacy policy and in-app disclosure,
    85	and requires a threat-model update.
    86	
    87	## App Store privacy commitments
    88	
    89	Before enabling the VPN, the iOS app MUST present what data is processed, where
    90	traffic exits, that the user controls the SSH host, and that the exit host can
    91	observe destination metadata. The privacy policy MUST state that Relux Works
    92	does not sell, use, or disclose VPN traffic data to third parties and describe
    93	retention/deletion for profile and support data.
    94	
    95	Regional VPN licensing and distribution availability are product/legal gates,
    96	not assumptions delegated to code.
    97	
    98	## Abuse boundaries
    99	
   100	The product connects only to infrastructure configured by the user. It does not
   101	scan for SSH servers, provide shared exit credentials, relay inbound unsolicited
   102	connections, or expose a server marketplace. Rate/resource limits protect both
   103	the device and exit host from accidental exhaustion.
    60	Exit: the validation matrix passes without routing loops, ordinary DNS leaks,
    61	unbounded buffers, or avoidable extension termination.
    62	
    63	## M4 — product and release (4–7 days)
    64	
    65	- Complete profile/key/host-trust UX, accessibility, diagnostics, privacy
    66	  disclosure, support export, and settings.
    67	- Finish macOS signing/notarization and iOS archive/TestFlight pipelines.
    68	- Generate relay manifest, third-party notices, SBOM, checksums, stable macOS
    69	  asset, release notes, and App Review package.
    70	- Run release-candidate regression on the supported device/OS matrix.
    71	
    72	Exit: reviewed, signed release candidates install through intended channels and
    73	the App Review submission is complete.
    74	
    75	## Critical dependencies
    76	
    77	```text
    78	A0/P0 platform viability
    79	  -> M0 packet bridge + M0 SSH engine
    80	  -> M1 TCP/DNS system VPN
    81	  -> M2 relay/UDP/degraded mode
    82	  -> M3 resilience/lane pool
    83	  -> M4 distribution
    84	```
    85	
    86	UI/profile foundations that do not encode unproven transport assumptions may
    87	overlap late M0/M1. Release and privacy work starts early as specifications and
    88	CI scaffolding but cannot pass until behavior is stable.
    89	
    90	## Explicit deferrals
    91	
    92	ProxyJump/OpenSSH config import, password-only auth, shared exit services,
    93	Android/Windows clients, traffic obfuscation, fake DNS, selective per-app VPN,
    94	and advanced policy routing are not baseline milestones. They require separate
    95	product approval and board epics.

